  "process": [
    "Collected seventeen seasons of nflverse play-by-play.",
    "Joined play-by-play, rosters, weekly injury reports, and snap counts.",
    "Labeled injury plays from descriptions, then removed descriptions from model inputs.",
    "Cleaned roster, position, surface, roof, weather, and status labels.",
    "Engineered only pre-snap context and play-type features.",
    "Locked a pre-registered comparison plan before viewing those results.",
    "One-hot encoded categories and standardized model inputs.",
    "Trained balanced logistic models for 120 historical windows.",
    "Tested every window on the immediately following season.",
    "Refit an unweighted model for calibrated browser predictions.",
    "Read coefficients as associations and checked uncertainty before making claims."
  ],
  "disclosures": [
    "This is an independent portfolio analysis, not an NFL or nflverse product.",
    "No result should be used to diagnose a player or make an individual medical decision.",
    "The personal introduction remains a marked placeholder for the author to write."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "These are associations, not proof that a field condition or play type causes an injury.",
    "Public descriptions and injury-report conventions changed across seasons.",
    "Body part was available for only about 30% of parsed injury-player events.",
    "The interactive model is calibrated on recent public data, not a clinical risk score.",
    "A recorded injury is not every injury that happened on the field."
  ],
  "lessons": [
    "The hardest modeling problem was defining a trustworthy label.",
    "A high join rate is evidence; an unmatched row is also evidence.",
    "Rule changes can alter exposure before they alter per-play risk.",
    "Stable aggregate rates can hide changing data definitions.",
    "Pre-registering comparisons makes null results worth reporting.",
    "Calibration and ranking answer different questions."
  ]
}